  "text_sanskrit": "बलं बलवतां चाहं कामरागविवर्जितम् । धर्माविरुद्धो भूतेषु कामोऽस्मि भरतर्षभ ॥ ७-११ ॥",
  "itrans": "balaM balavatA.n chAhaM kAmarAgavivarjitam . dharmAviruddho bhUteShu kAmo.asmi bharatarShabha .. 7-11 ..",
  "translation": "And I am the strength of the strong, the strength which is destitute of Kama and Raga; in beings I am the Kama, which is not opposed to Dharma, O the foremost of the Bharata race. (11)\nV. Of the strong: In the strong I am present, and I am the strength free from Kama and Raga: Since My strength does not depend upon nourishment from objects enjoyed, but it is eternal and absolutely perfect, I am the strength--unmixed of Kama or desires. Since it is not used for improper objects, it is the strengths free from Raga or passion, for indeed the passionate make use of their strength in improper places. The desire or Kama in beings which is not opposed to Dharma or righteousness — that Kama I am.\nIn the phrases, pure fragrance, the strength destitute of Kama and Raga, &c. pure and other epithets signify that the Lord is to be contemplated as present in the best things alone, but not in the disagreeable or objectionable properties.\nA. The Lord is the ordainer not only of Rasa and other properties, but of all things, water, &c, which the Lord summarises as follows:",
